<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Schwarzburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1123 CE – 1599 CE (approx.) · capital Arnstadt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Thuringian county whose counts briefly gave Germany a rival king and whose two branches survived as principalities until 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sizzo-iii-of-schwarzburg">Sizzo III</a>, who held lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>, called himself count of Schwarzburg in 1123 after the castle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schwarza-saale">Schwarza</a> river. The family held scattered lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolstadt">Rudolstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>, expanding through marriage and purchase.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were among the leading Thuringian nobles alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettins</a>. In 1349 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunther-von-schwarzburg">Günther XXI of Schwarzburg</a> was elected anti-king against Charles IV but resigned within months and died the same year. The family split into many lines, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> was adopted in the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1599 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stadtilm">Treaty of Stadtilm</a> divided the lands into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-rudolstadt">Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</a>, which became principalities in 1697 and 1710. Both lasted until 1918 and became part of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> in 1920.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-schwarzburg.jpg" alt="County of Schwarzburg" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Nomadic1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CoA_Schwarzburg_County.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1123 CE – 1599 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arnstadt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sizzo III, Günther XXI of Schwarzburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thuringian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schwarzburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1361&amp;focus=county-of-schwarzburg" aria-label="County of Schwarzburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4929 414 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Schwarzburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5249 670L5229 694M5249 670L5258 673M5249 670L5246 655"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5258 673h0M5246 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5249 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5249 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arnstadt. <a href="/atlas?year=1361&amp;focus=county-of-schwarzburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
